WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.500
Define the regional goal. Decide whether another region is for disaster recovery, nearby reads or active writes. Each operation can have different latency, availability and consistency requirements.

2
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:26.083
Copy suitable state. Region A sends changes toward Region B. Asynchronous copying can lag; synchronous coordination adds waiting and depends on the communication path.

3
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:37.417
Lose the connection. The regions cannot communicate. Allowing both to confirm the same last seat independently can violate correctness even though both applications are running.

4
00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:48.417
Fence and promote. A safe failover establishes which writer owns new decisions and prevents the old writer from conflicting. Routing alone does not settle database ownership.

5
00:00:48.417 --> 00:01:02.167
Route and validate. Direct eligible traffic to the recovery region and validate the actual booking workflow. Its capacity, credentials and dependencies must work independently enough for the failure scenario.

6
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:15.583
Plan reconciliation and failback. After recovery, reconcile lag and in-flight work before moving ownership back. Test both failover and failback instead of treating the first traffic switch as the whole plan.
